const body = `<h1>FRQ 1: Methods and Control Structures</h1>

<p>
    7 points, two parts. Part A (4 points): iteration and conditionals. Part
    B (3 points): String methods. You write two methods of a given class,
    from specifications and examples. This is the question your whole fall
    trained for: if you can roll dice, parse commands, and loop rooms, you
    can do this.
</p>

<h2>Round A, Part A: Dice Duel (4 points)</h2>

<div class="callout lab">
<p>A <code>Duel</code> class is given. Two fighters each roll a die.
<strong>Advantage rules:</strong> if a fighter's roll is exactly 6, they roll
again and add. This can repeat. A fighter is "victorious" when their total is
at least 10 and strictly greater than the opponent's total.</p>

<p>Write the method:</p>
<pre><code>/** Simulates one duel. Rolls for fighter A and fighter B,
 *  applying the explode-on-6 rule, and returns "A", "B", or "draw".
 */
public String duel(int sides)</code></pre>

<p>Example: <code>duel(6)</code> might produce A rolls 6 then 4 = 10, B rolls
3 then 5 = 8, returns "A". A duel where both tie at 10 returns "draw".</p>
</div>

<h3>Solution</h3>
<pre><code>public String duel(int sides) {
    int totalA = rollExplode(sides);
    int totalB = rollExplode(sides);
    if (totalA == totalB) return "draw";
    if (totalA &gt; totalB &amp;&amp; totalA &gt;= 10) return "A";
    if (totalB &gt; totalA &amp;&amp; totalB &gt;= 10) return "B";
    return "draw";               // neither reached 10
}

private int rollExplode(int sides) {
    int total = 0;
    int roll;
    do {
        roll = (int)(Math.random() * sides) + 1;
        total += roll;
    } while (roll == sides);
    return total;
}</code></pre>

<p>Commentary: the helper method is exam-legal and rubric-friendly. The
"victorious" definition has THREE conditions hiding in it (greater, at least
10, tie handling). Every rubric row tests one of them.</p>

<h3>Rubric (4 points)</h3>
<table>
    <tr><th>Point</th><th>Earned by</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Loop that rolls and accumulates a total (either
        fighter)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Explode rule: re-roll condition correct (roll == max,
        not total)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Comparison logic: strict greater between fighters</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Threshold 10 and draw cases both handled</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Round A, Part B: The Whisper Filter (3 points)</h2>

<div class="callout lab">
<p>Write the method:</p>
<pre><code>/** Returns a "whisper" version of message: lowercase, with all
 *  vowels removed. If message is empty, returns "...".
 */
public String whisper(String message)</code></pre>

<p>Examples: <code>whisper("ATTACK THE GOBLIN")</code> returns
"ttck th gbln". <code>whisper("")</code> returns "...".</p>
</div>

<h3>Solution</h3>
<pre><code>public String whisper(String message) {
    if (message.length() == 0) return "...";
    String result = "";
    String lower = message.toLowerCase();   // wait: not on the JQR!
    ...
}</code></pre>

<p>Stop. <code>toLowerCase</code> is NOT on the Java Quick Reference, and the
real exam will not require it. The exam-safe version handles case the way the
exam does, by comparing against both cases or by processing only characters
you are told about. Exam-safe solution:</p>

<pre><code>public String whisper(String message) {
    if (message.length() == 0) return "...";
    String result = "";
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; message.length(); i++) {
        String ch = message.substring(i, i + 1);
        if (!ch.equals("a") &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("e") &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("i")
                &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("o") &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("u")
                &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("A") &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("E") &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("I")
                &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("O") &amp;&amp; !ch.equals("U")) {
            result += ch;
        }
    }
    return result;
}</code></pre>

<p>Commentary: substring-by-one is the exam's char idiom when charAt is not
offered. The ten-way comparison is ugly and completely correct. Ugly and
correct beats elegant and incomplete.</p>

<h3>Rubric (3 points)</h3>
<table>
    <tr><th>Point</th><th>Earned by</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Empty-string check returning "..."</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Loop over all characters (or substring positions)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Correct vowel test and accumulation into a result
        String</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="roundb">Round B (mock exam material)</h2>

<div class="callout lab">
<p><strong>B1, Potion Timer (4 points):</strong> A potion is "active" for
exactly 3 minutes after drinking. Write
<code>public int dosesNeeded(int minutes)</code> that returns how many potions
cover <code>minutes</code> of effect (any partial minute counts as a full
potion). Examples: dosesNeeded(3) is 1, dosesNeeded(4) is 2, dosesNeeded(0)
is 0. Guard: negative input returns 0.</p>

<p><strong>B2, Shield Bash (3 points):</strong> Write
<code>public String bashReport(String enemy)</code> that returns the enemy
name repeated once per letter of <code>enemy.length()</code>... capped at 5
repeats, joined by "-". Examples: bashReport("orc") returns "orc-orc-orc",
bashReport("dragon") returns "dragon-dragon-dragon-dragon-dragon-dragon"?
No: capped at 5, so "dragon" (6 letters) returns 5 repeats joined by "-".
bashReport("") returns "".</p>
</div>

<p>Solutions and rubrics for Round B are discussed in the May 4 rubric
workshop, not posted. That is on purpose: the mock is for measuring, the
workshop is for learning, and reading solutions before attempting is
measuring nothing.</p>

<h2>Drill List</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Write rollExplode from memory. Three times. Until it is boring.</li>
    <li>JQR row check: substring(from, to). What is to's role? Say it in
        words before looking.</li>
    <li>Empty-string and single-character edge cases: every String method
        question on the exam hides one.</li>
</ul>`;
